Declaration(Class(goldbook:16199))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16199 "caged compound")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16199 "Molecular species that can rapidly be converted from inactive into active form by light. Typically, photocaged compounds have a covalently attached group that can be photocleaved by electromagnetic radiation of a specific wavelength.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16199 "Electromagnetic radiation activated caged compounds (photocages) are widely used in biochemistry for a rapid photoinduced introduction of a variety of compounds to biological systems with spatial and temporal control, allowing the time-resolved study of the ensuing events."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16199 "An example is the photorelease of nucleotides from their attachment to 1-(4,5-dimethoxy-2-nitrophenyl) ethyl ester."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:16199 goldbook:16214)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16199 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16199 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16200))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16200 "charge-transfer absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16200 "Electronic absorption corresponding to a charge-transfer transition.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:16200 "CT")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:16200 "CT absorption")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16200 "In some cases, the charge-transfer absorption band(s) may be strongly obscured by the local absorptions of the donor and acceptor systems."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16200 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16200 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16201))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16201 "cis/trans photoisomerisation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16201 "Photoinduced geometric isomerization about a double bond.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16201 "Geometrical isomerization of a \\(\\ce{C-C}\\) double bond is called a cis/trans isomerization in 1,2-disubstituted alkenes. \\(E/Z\\) isomerization is a more general designation applying also to higher substituted alkenes."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16201 "Photochemical pathways for cis/trans isomerization have the advantage over thermal and catalytic methods of giving equilibrium cis/trans mixtures (photostationary states) rich in thermodynamically unstable isomers."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16201 "In a \\(\\ce{C-C}\\) double bond, a geometrical cis/trans (or \\(E/Z\\)) isomerization implies a \\(\\pu{180\\!^{\\circ}}\\) rotation (except when modified by steric interactions), whereas in other bonds the mechanism might be different, e.g., in azocompounds, a cis/trans photoisomerization may occur through an inversion at a \\(\\ce{N}\\) center."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16201 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16201 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16202))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16202 "external quantum efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16202 "Efficiency of light emission for solid-state light-emitting devices, such as organic light-emitting devices (OLEDs). Analogous to emission quantum yield.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16202 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16202 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16203))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16203 "Franck–Condon factor")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16203 "Squared modulus of the overlap integral between the vibrational wave functions (\\(\\mathit{\\Theta}\\)) for the initial (0) and final (\\(\\rm{e}\\)) state of a given electronic transition. \\[\\left\\vert \\int \\mathit{\\Theta} _{\\rm{v'}}^{(\\rm{e})} \\mathit{\\Theta} _{\\rm{v}}^{(0)} dQ \\right\\vert^{2}\\] where the integration is over all nuclear coordinates.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16203 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16203 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16205))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:16205 "Lippert–Mataga equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:16205 "Correlation of the Stokes-shift, \\(\\Delta \\tilde{ u}\\), between the absorption and emission maxima, and the orientation polarizability \\(f(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}},n)\\) of the solvent,\\[\\Delta \\tilde{ u} = 2 \\frac{(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{exc}} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{gr}})^{2}}{c h r^{3}} f(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}},n) + {\\rm{const.}}\\]with \\(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{exc}}\\) and \\(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{gr}}\\) the excited- and ground-state dipole moments of the dissolved molecule, respectively, \\(c\\) the speed of light, \\(h\\) the Planck constant, \\(r\\) the radius of the cavity in which the solute resides, and \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\) the 'relative static permittivity' (formerly called dielectric constant). The expression of the Stokes shift depends only on the absolute magnitude of the difference between dipole moments and not on the angle between the dipoles. The above equation is sometimes called the Lippert equation. The orientation polarizability is given by\\[f(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}},n) = \\frac{(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}} - 1)}{(2\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}} + 1)} - \\frac{(n^{2} - 1)}{(2n^{2} + 1)}\\]with \\(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}}\\) the relative static permittivity and \\(n\\) the refractive index of the solvent, \\(f(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}},n)\\) ranges from \\(\\pu{0.001}\\) in cyclohexane to \\(\\pu{0.320}\\) in water.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16205 "\\(\\Delta \\tilde{\\nu}\\) as a function of \\(f(\\varepsilon_{\\rm{r}},n)\\) (Lippert plot), is used to determine the difference in dipole moment (\\(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{exc}} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\rm{gr}}\\)) between a molecule in the equilibrated excited state and in the ground state, as a measure of the change in charge distribution brought about by light absorption. In this method, it is assumed that the dipole moments do not depend on solvent polarity. A linear variation is not always observed because only dipole-dipole interactions are taken into account and the solute polarizability is neglected."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:16205 "The assumptions made in theories of solvatochromic shifts, together with the uncertainty over the size and shape of the cavity radius \\(r\\), explain the inaccuracies in the determined excited-state dipole moments."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:16205 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:16205 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:16206))
