Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00079 "acidity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00079 "Of a compound: For Brønsted acids the tendency of a compound to act as a hydron donor. It can be quantitatively expressed by the acid dissociation constant of the compound in water or some other specified medium. For Lewis acids it relates to the association constants of Lewis adducts and π adducts.Of a medium: The use of the term is mainly restricted to a medium containing Brønsted acids, where it means the tendency of the medium to hydronate a specific reference base. It is quantitatively expressed by the appropriate acidity function.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:B00744)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:L03508)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:L03510)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:A00081)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:A00472)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:D01801)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:H02904)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00079 goldbook:A04388)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00079 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00079 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00081 "acidity function")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00081 "Any function that measures the thermodynamic hydron-donating or -accepting ability of a solvent system, or a closely related thermodynamic property, such as the tendency of the lyate ion of the solvent system to form Lewis adduct. (The term 'basicity function' is not in common use in connection with basic solutions.) Acidity functions are not unique properties of the solvent system alone, but depend on the solute (or family of closely related solutes) with respect to which the thermodynamic tendency is measured. Commonly used acidity functions refer to concentrated acidic or basic solutions. Acidity functions are usually established over a range of composition of such a system by UV/VIS spectrophotometric or NMR measurements of the degree of hydronation (protonation or Lewis adduct formation) for the members of a series of structurally similar indicator bases (or acids) of different strength: the best known of these functions is the Hammett acidity function \\(H_{0}\\) (for uncharged indicator bases that are primary aromatic amines).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00081 goldbook:A00079)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00081 goldbook:L03510)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00081 goldbook:A00274)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00081 goldbook:H02904)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00081 goldbook:L03653)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00081 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00081 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00082 "acidosis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00082 "Pathological condition in which the hydrogen ion substance concentration of body fluids is above normal and hence the pH of blood falls below the reference interval.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00082 goldbook:A00221)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00082 goldbook:S06072)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00082 doi:10.1351/pac199365092003)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00082 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00083 "acid rain")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00083 "Rain with \\(\\rm{pH}\\) values &lt; about 5; commonly results from acids formed from pollutants. 'Pure' rain water equilibrated with atmospheric $\\ce{CO2}$ and naturally occurring acids in relatively clean air usually has a \\(\\rm{pH} > 5\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00083 doi:10.1351/pac199062112167)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00083 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00085 "<i>aci</i>-nitro compounds")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00085 "A class name for hydrocarbylideneazinic acids, $\\ce{R2C=N^{+}(–O^{−})OH}$. The use of aci-nitro as a prefix in systematic nomenclature to name specific compounds is abandoned.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00085 goldbook:A00558)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00085 doi:10.1351/pac199567081307)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00085 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00086 "actinic flux")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00086 "The quantity of light available to molecules at a particular point in the atmosphere and which, on absorption, drives photochemical processes in the atmosphere. It is calculated by integrating the spectral radiance \\(L \\left(\\lambda,\\,\\theta,\\,\\varphi\\,\\right)\\) over all directions of incidence of the light, \\(S_{\\lambda} = \\int _{\\theta}\\, \\int _{\\phi} \\{L\\left (\\lambda,\\theta,\\varphi \\right)/(hc/\\lambda)\\}\\, \\rm{sin}\\,\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\varphi\\). If the radiance is expressed in \\(\\pu{J m-2 s-1 st-1 nm-1}\\) and \\(hc/\\lambda\\) is the energy per quantum of light of wavelength \\(\\lambda\\), the actinic flux has units of \\(\\rm{quanta cm}^{-2}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\ \\rm{nm}^{-1}\\). This important quantity is one of the terms required in the calculation of j-values, the first order rate coefficients for photochemical processes in the sunlight-absorbing, trace gases in the atmosphere. The actinic flux is determined by the solar radiation entering the atmosphere and by any changes in this due to atmospheric gases and particles (e.g. Rayleigh scattering absorption by stratospheric ozone, scattering and absorption by aerosols and clouds), and reflections from the ground. It is therefore dependent on the wavelength of the light, on the altitude and on specific local environmental conditions. The actinic flux has borne many names (e.g. flux, flux density, beam irradiance, actinic irradiance, integrated intensity) which has caused some confusion. It is important to distinguish the actinic flux from the spectral irradiance, which refers to energy arrival on a flat surface having fixed spatial orientation (\\(\\pu{J m-2 nm-1}\\)) given by: \\[E_{\\lambda} = \\int _{\\theta}\\, \\int _{\\phi} L\\left (\\lambda,\\theta,\\varphi \\right )\\, \\rm{cos}\\,\\theta  \\: \\rm{sin}\\,\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\theta\\: \\rm{d}\\varphi\\] The actinic flux does not refer to any specific orientation because molecules are oriented randomly in the atmosphere. This distinction is of practical relevance: the actinic flux (and therefore a j-value) near a brightly reflecting surface (e.g. over snow or above a thick cloud) can be a factor of three higher than that near a non-reflecting surface. The more descriptive name of spectral spheradiance is suggested for the quantity herein called actinic flux.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:R05160)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:AT07314)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:E02106)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:P04627)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:R05037)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:S05487)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:S05817)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:S05824)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:S05832)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00086 goldbook:W06659)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00086 doi:10.1351/pac199062112167)
