Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00087))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00087 "actinometer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00087 "A chemical system or physical device which determines the number of photons in a beam integrally or per unit time. This name is commonly applied to devices used in the ultraviolet and visible wavelength ranges. For example, solutions of iron(III) oxalate can be used as a chemical actinometer, while bolometers, thermopiles, and photodiodes are physical devices giving a reading that can be correlated to the number of photons detected.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:C01016)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:P04627)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:R05189)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:UT07492)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:VT07496)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00087 goldbook:W06659)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00087 doi:10.1351/pac199668122223)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00087 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00088))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00088 "action spectrum")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00088 "A plot of a relative biological or chemical photoresponse (= \\(\\Delta y\\)) per number of incident photons against wavelength or energy of radiation under the same radiant power of light. This form of presentation is frequently used in the studies of biological or solid state systems, where the nature of the absorbing species is unknown. This type of action spectrum is sometimes called spectral responsivity or sensitivity spectrum. The precise action spectrum is a plot of the spectral or quantum effectiveness. By contrast, a plot of the biological or chemical change or response per absorbed photon (quantum efficiency) versus wavelength is the efficiency spectrum.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:E01908)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:E02253)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:Q04988)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:R05046)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:S05814)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:S05829)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00088 goldbook:W06659)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00088 doi:10.1351/pac199668122223)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00088 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00089))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00089 "activated adsorption process")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00089 "If the temperature coefficient of the rate of adsorption is substantial, an adsorption process is said to be activated (i.e. to have a significant activation energy). In this case, the sticking coefficient is small. In general, the activation energy of activated adsorption is a function of coverage and it usually increases with increasing coverage.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00089 goldbook:A00093)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00089 goldbook:A00102)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00089 goldbook:A00155)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00089 goldbook:C01124)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00089 goldbook:S06012)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00089 doi:10.1351/pac197646010071)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00089 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00090))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00090 "activated carbon")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00090 "A porous carbon material, a char which has been subjected to reaction with gases, sometimes with the addition of chemicals, e.g. $\\ce{ZnCl2}$, before, during or after carbonization in order to increase its adsorptive properties.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:A00090 "Activated carbons have a large adsorption capacity, preferably for small molecules, and are used for purification of liquids and gases. By controlling the process of carbonization and activation, a variety of active carbons having different porosity can be obtained. Activated carbons are used mainly in granular and powdered forms, but can also be produced in textile form by controlled carbonization and activation of textile fibres. Other terms used in the literature: active carbons, active charcoals."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:A00093)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:A00156)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:A00166)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:C00841)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:C00840)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:C00974)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:A00090 goldbook:P04762)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:A00090 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:A00090 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:A00091))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:A00091 "activated charcoal")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:A00091 "A traditional term for activated carbon.")
