Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00822))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00822 "carbonaceous mesophase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00822 "A liquid-crystalline state of pitch which shows the optical birefringence of disc-like (discotic) nematic liquid crystals. It can be formed as an intermediate phase during thermolysis (pyrolysis) of an isotropic molten pitch or by precipitation from pitch fractions prepared by selective extraction. Generally, the spherical mesophase precipitated from a pyrolysing pitch has the Brooks and Taylor structure. With continuous heat treatment the carbonaceous mesophase coalesces to a state of bulk mesophase before solidification to green coke with further loss of hydrogen or low-molecular-weight compounds.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00822 "In the formation of carbonaceous mesophase by thermolysis (pyrolysis) of isotropic molten pitch, the development of a liquid-crystalline phase is accompanied by simultaneous aromatic polymerization reactions. The reactivity of pitch with increasing heat treatment temperature and its thermosetting nature are responsible for the lack of a true reversible thermotropic phase transition for the bulk mesophase in most pitches. Due to its glass-like nature most of the liquid-crystalline characteristics are retained in the super-cooled solid state."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:B00747)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:B00754)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:E02301)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:G02697)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:LT06855)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:M03849)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:P04537)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:P04677)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:P04740)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:P04795)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:P04961)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:S05733)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:T06326)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00822 goldbook:W06668)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00822 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00822 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00823))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00823 "carbon artifact")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00823 "An 'artificially produced' solid body which consists mainly of carbonaceous material in a distinct shape.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00823 "Sometimes this term is also used for artificially (in the sense of technically) produced non-shaped carbon materials such as coke, blacks, etc. This application of the term carbon artifact is not recommended. Synonyms to the term carbon artifact are: 'artificial carbon article' or 'artificial carbon body'."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00823 goldbook:C00841)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00823 goldbook:C01142)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00823 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00823 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00825))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00825 "carbon–carbon composite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00825 "A carbon fibre-reinforced carbon matrix material. The carbon matrix phase is typically formed by solid, liquid or gaseous pyrolysis of an organic precursor material. The matrix is either a graphitizable carbon or non-graphitizable carbon, and the carbonaceous reinforcement is fibrous in form. The composite may also contain other components in particulate or fibrous forms.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00825 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00825 goldbook:CT07545)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00825 goldbook:G02690)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00825 goldbook:N04194)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00825 goldbook:P04961)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00825 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00825 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00826))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00826 "carbon cenospheres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00826 "Porous or hollow carbonaceous sphere-like particles (frequently in the size range of a few to several hundreds of µm diameter) formed during pyrolysis, also in the course of combustion, of carbonaceous liquid droplets (e.g. heavy fuel) or solid particles (e.g. coal).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00826 goldbook:P04961)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00826 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00826 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00827))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00827 "carbon cloth")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00827 "A textile material consisting of carbon fibres oriented at least in two directions. Carbon cloth is not necessarily woven.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00827 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00827 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00827 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00828))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00828 "carbon dioxide laser")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00828 "A continuous or pulsed source of coherent radiation normally tunable through the $\\ce{CO2}$ vibration–rotation band centred near \\(10.6\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00828 "CO<sub>2</sub> laser")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00828 goldbook:C01130)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00828 goldbook:G02585)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00828 goldbook:L03459)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00828 doi:10.1351/pac199668122223)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00828 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00829))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00829 "carbon electrode")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00829 "An electrode for an electrical application. In its green state it comprises granular carbon material bound with pitch. The granular carbon material may be either needle coke, fine-grained or isotropic coke or reclaimed graphite powder. Electrodes for use in steel production can only be manufactured from needle coke, and the green electrodes are fired at temperatures above around \\(2800\\ \\rm{K}\\) to produce highly graphite electrodes. The other granular carbon materials may be used for aluminium electrodes where the duty is not so severe, and the green electrodes are generally fired to lower temperatures.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00829 "In both cases it is essential that the granular carbons and the pitch binders used in the production of the green electrodes have a low sulfur content as the release of sulfur during the high-temperature firing can lead to the production of significant porosity."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:C01142)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:G02682)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:G02684)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:G02689)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:G02692)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:I03354)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:N04095)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:P04677)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:P04762)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00829 goldbook:P04941)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00829 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00829 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00830))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00830 "carbon felt")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00830 "A textile material consisting of, in approximation, randomly oriented and intertwined carbon fibres.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00830 "Carbon felts are usually fabricated by carbonization of organic felts but they can also be produced from short carbon fibres."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00830 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00830 goldbook:C00840)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00830 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00830 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00831))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00831 "carbon fibre")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00831 "Fibres (filaments, tows, yarns, rovings) consisting of at least $\\pu{92\\%}$ (mass fraction) carbon, usually in the non-graphitic state.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00831 "Carbon fibres are fabricated by pyrolysis of organic precursor fibres or by growth from gaseous hydrocarbons. The use of the term graphite fibres instead of carbon fibres as often observed in the literature is incorrect and should be avoided. The term graphite fibres is justified only if three-dimensional crystalline order is confirmed, e.g. by X-ray diffraction measurements."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:C00821)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:D01711)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:G02686)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:H02889)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:M03722)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00831 goldbook:P04961)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00831 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00831 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00832))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00832 "carbon fibre fabrics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00832 "Woven textile materials made of carbon fibres.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00832 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00832 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00832 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00833))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00833 "carbon fibres type HM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00833 "Carbon fibres type HM (high modulus) are carbon fibres with a value of Young's modulus (tensile modulus) larger than \\(300\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) (nearly $\\pu{30\\%}$ of the \\(C_{11}\\) elastic constant of a graphite single crystal).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00833 "The level of the tensile modulus of carbon fibres is controlled by the degree of preferred orientation of the layer planes in the direction parallel to the fibre axis. \\(C_{11}\\), the elastic constant of graphite single crystals in the direction of the layer planes, is \\(1060\\ \\pm 20\\ \\rm{GPa}\\). In general, the ratio of tensile strength to tensile modulus is smaller than \\(1 \\times 10^{-2}\\) for carbon fibres type HM (but the tensile strength is influenced by flaws in the fibres and may be improved in the future). Carbon fibres type UHM (ultra-high modulus) have moduli of elasticity in excess of \\(600\\ \\rm{GPa}\\), surpassing 50% of the theoretical \\(C_{11}\\) number. Such high values of Young's modulus can be achieved most readily in mesophase pitch-based carbon fibres (MPP-based carbon fibres)."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00833 goldbook:M04038)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00833 goldbook:Y06733)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00833 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00833 goldbook:G02684)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00833 goldbook:M03851)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00833 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00833 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00834))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00834 "carbon fibres type HT")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00834 "Carbon fibres type HT are carbon fibres with values of Young's modulus between \\(150\\) and \\(275\\) to \\(300\\ \\rm{GPa}\\). The term HT, referring to high tensile strength, was early applied because fibres of this type display the highest tensile strengths.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00834 "HT")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00834 "The disposition of boundaries between the fibre types is somewhat arbitrary. For carbon fibres type HT, the values of the strength-to-stiffness ratio are typically larger than \\(1.5 \\times 10^{-2}\\). The tensile strength of carbon fibres is flaw-controlled, however, and therefore the measured values increase strongly as the diameter of the filaments is decreased."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00834 goldbook:M03966)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00834 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00834 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00834 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00835))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00835 "carbon fibres type IM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00835 "Carbon fibres type IM (intermediate modulus) are related to carbon fibres type HT because of the comparable values of tensile strength, but are characterized by greater stiffness (Young's modulus up to approximately $\\pu{35\\%}$ of the theoretical \\(C_{11}\\) value).")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00835 "IM")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00835 "The tensile modulus (Young's modulus) varies between ca. \\(275\\) and \\(350\\ \\rm{GPa}\\), but the disposition of the boundaries is somewhat arbitrary. The relatively high ratio of tensile strength to tensile modulus, typically above \\(1 \\times 10^{-2}\\), in carbon fibres type IM, in spite of an increase of Young's modulus, requires a further increase of strength, which is achievable by a significant reduction of the monofilament diameter down to about \\(5\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\). Such small filament diameters are typical of carbon fibres type IM."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00835 goldbook:M03966)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00835 goldbook:C00834)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00835 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00835 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00836))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00836 "carbon fibres type LM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00836 "Carbon fibres type LM (low modulus) are carbon fibres with isotropic structure, tensile modulus values as low as $\\pu{10\\%}$ of the \\(C_{11}\\) values of the graphite single crystal, and low strength values.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00836 "LM")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00836 "The term carbon fibres type LM is sometimes used for various types of isotropic carbon fibres known as pitch-based or rayon-based carbon fibres that have not been subjected to hot-stretching. Such fibres are not used for reinforcement purposes in high-performance composites."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:G02684)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:I03354)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:P04678)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00836 goldbook:R05161)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00836 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00836 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00837))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00837 "carbon fibres type UHM")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00837 "Carbon fibres type UHM (ultra-high modulus) designates a class of carbon fibres having very high values of Young's modulus larger than \\(600\\ \\rm{GPa}\\) (i.e. greater than $\\pu{55\\%}$ of the theoretical \\(C_{11}\\) value of graphite).")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:C00837 "UHM")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00837 goldbook:M03966)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00837 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00837 goldbook:G02684)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00837 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00837 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00838))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00838 "carbonitriles")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00838 "In systematic nomenclature, the suffix -carbonitrile is used to name compounds $\\ce{RC#N}$ where the suffix includes the carbon atom of the $\\ce{–CN}$. However, carbonitrile is not a class name for nitriles.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00838 goldbook:N04151)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00838 doi:10.1351/pac199567081307)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00838 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00839))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00839 "carbonium ion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00839 "The term should be used with great care since several incompatible meanings are currently in use. It is not acceptable as the root for systematic nomenclature for carbocation.In most of the existing literature the term is used in its traditional sense for what is here defined as carbenium ion.A carbocation, real or hypothetical, that contains at least one five-coordinate carbon atom.A carbocation, real or hypothetical, whose structure cannot adequately be described by two-electron two-centre bonds only. (The structure may involve carbon atoms with a coordination number greater than five.)")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00839 goldbook:C00812)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00839 goldbook:C00817)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00839 goldbook:C01331)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00839 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00839 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00840))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00840 "carbonization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00840 "A process by which solid residues with increasing content of the element carbon are formed from organic material usually by pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00840 "As with all pyrolytic reactions, carbonization is a complex process in which many reactions take place concurrently such as dehydrogenation, condensation, hydrogen transfer and isomerization. It differs from coalification in that its reaction rate is faster by many orders of magnitude. The final pyrolysis temperature applied controls the degree of carbonization and the residual content of foreign elements, e.g. at \\(T \\sim 1200\\ \\rm{K}\\) the carbon content of the residue exceeds a mass fraction of 90 wt.%, whereas at \\(T \\sim 1600\\ \\rm{K}\\) more than 99 wt.% carbon is found."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00840 goldbook:C01120)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00840 goldbook:I03026)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00840 goldbook:I03295)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00840 goldbook:M03722)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00840 goldbook:P04961)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00840 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00840 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00841))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00841 "carbon material")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00841 "A solid high in content of the element carbon and structurally in a non-graphitic state.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:C00841 "The use of the term carbon as a short term for a material consisting of non-graphitic carbon is incorrect. The use of the term carbon without a second noun or a clarifying adjective should be restricted to the chemical element carbon. The term carbon can be used in combination with other nouns or clarifying adjectives for special types of carbon materials (carbon electrode, carbon fibres, pyrolytic carbon, glass-like carbon and others)."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:C00821)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:C00829)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:C01022)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:G02639)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:N04193)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00841 goldbook:P04963)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C00841 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C00841 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C00842))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C00842 "carbon mix")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C00842 "A mixture of filler coke, e.g. grains and/or powders of solid carbon materials and a carbonaceous binder and selected additives, prepared in heated mixers at temperatures in the range of \\(410{-}445\\ \\rm{K}\\) as a preliminary step for the formation of shaped green bodies.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00842 goldbook:B00646)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00842 goldbook:C00841)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C00842 goldbook:F02364)
