Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01452))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01452 "current density")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01452 "The current density \\(j_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) of a species B in a given point of the solution is obtained by multiplying the flux density of that species at the given point by the Faraday constant \\(F\\) and by the charge number \\(z_{\\rm{B}}\\) of the species: \\[j_{\\mathbf{B}} = z_{\\rm{B}}\\ F\\ N_{\\mathbf{B}}\\] where \\(j_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) is a vector which indicates the direction in which the charges transported by the species B flow and which gives the number of these charges going through a plane oriented perpendicular to the vector, divided by time and by area, and \\(N_{\\mathbf{B}}\\) is the flux density of a minor constituent of the solution with respect to a fixed frame of reference.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01452 goldbook:F02325)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01452 goldbook:C00993)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01452 goldbook:C01281)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01452 goldbook:E01928)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C01452 doi:10.1351/pac198153101827)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C01452 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01456 "current distribution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01456 "The ratio of current density at a point X on an interface to the average current density (\\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j}\\)) is called the 'relative local current density'. The current distribution is described by the function \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x)\\) (or more generally, \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x,\\,y,\\,z)\\) where \\(x\\) or \\((x,\\,y,\\,z)\\) are the coordinates of the points of the electrode-solution interface. The 'primary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of overpotential is negligible. The 'secondary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential cannot be neglected but concentration overpotential is negligible. The secondary distribution is often described in terms of dimensionless numbers of the form \\[\\mathrm{Wa}=\\frac{\\kappa }{l}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\eta }{\\mathrm{d}j}\\] where \\(\\kappa \\) is the conductivity of the solution, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}\\eta }{\\rm{d}j}\\) the slope of the overpotential-current curve under the above conditions and \\(l\\) a characteristic length of the system, for instance the radius of a disc electrode. \\(\\mathrm{Wa}\\) is the 'Wagner number'. It is a quantity which determines the throwing power and characterizes the equalizing influence of overpotential on the current distribution. In electroplating the 'throwing power' is qualitatively defined as 'the ability of a solution to deposit metal uniformly upon a cathode of irregular shape'. The 'tertiary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential (including concentration overpotential) cannot be neglected.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:A00539)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:C00905)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:C01230)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:C01245)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:C01452)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:I03082)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01456 goldbook:O04358)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C01456 doi:10.1351/pac198153101827)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C01456 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01458))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01458 "current efficiency")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01458 "If several reactions take place simultaneously at the electrode a partial electrode current density (c.d.) \\(j_{k}\\) can be assigned to each reaction. It is given by the stoichiometry of the reaction and by the amount of substance of B reacting (per unit time and per unit electrode area) in the reaction considered. The current efficiency of reaction \\(k\\), \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) is defined as the ratio of \\(j_{k}\\) to the total c.d.: \\[\\varepsilon_{k}=\\frac{j_{k}}{\\sum_{m} j_{m}}\\] Note that \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) may be larger than one if cathodic and anodic reactions take place simultaneously at the same electrode. However, \\(\\varepsilon_{k}\\) still gives correctly the product yield, which is the quantity of industrial interest. The product yield is the amount of substance of B produced per unit charge and is equal to \\(\\frac{\\varepsilon_{k}\\  u _{B,k}}{n_{k}\\ F}\\) (in the absence of a chemical reaction which is consecutive to the electrode reaction and which consumes or produces species B). \\(n_{k}\\) is the charge number of electrode reaction \\(k\\). Note that in the case of simultaneous electrode reactions the distribution of the partial c.d. \\(j_{k}\\) may be different from that of the total c.d., i.e. the function \\(\\frac{\\left(j_{k}\\right)_{\\rm{x}}}{j}=f_{k}\\left(x\\right)\\) may be different from \\(\\frac{j_{\\rm{x}}}{j} = f(x)\\). In electroplating the term 'metal distribution' is sometimes used to designate the distribution \\(f_{k}\\left(x\\right)\\) of the partial c.d. for metal deposition.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:A00297)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:C00993)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:C01033)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:C01452)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:E01960)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01458 goldbook:S06026)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C01458 doi:10.1351/pac198153101827)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C01458 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:C01480))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:C01480 "Curtin–Hammett principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:C01480 "In a chemical reaction that yields one product (X) from one conformational isomer (A') and a different product (Y) from another conformational isomer (A'') (and provided these two isomers are rapidly interconvertible relative to the rate of product formation, whereas the products do not undergo interconversion) the product composition is not in direct proportion to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers in the substrate; it is controlled only by the difference in standard free energies (\\(\\unicode[Times]{x3B4} \\Delta ^{\\ddagger }G\\)) of the respective transition states.  It is also true that the product composition is formally related to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers A' and A'' (i.e. the conformational equilibrium constant) and the respective rate constants of their reactions; these parameters are generally — though not invariably — unknown. The diagram below represents the energetic situation for transformation of interconverting isomers A and A' into products X and Y.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:C01033)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:E02177)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:I03289)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:S06082)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:T06446)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:C01480 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:C01480 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:C01480 "current"^^xsd:string)
