AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07249 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07267))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07267 "extender")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07267 "Substance, especially a diluent or modifier, added to a polymer to increase its volume without substantially altering the desirable properties of the polymer.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07267 "An extender may be a liquid or a solid."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07267 doi:10.1351/pac200476111985)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07267 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07367))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07367 "electron back-transfer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07367 "Thermal inversion of excited-state electron transfer restoring the donor and acceptor in their original oxidation state. In using this term one should also specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor. This term is preferred to back electron-transfer.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07367 "It is recommended to use this term only for the process restoring the original electronic state of donor and acceptor."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07367 "Should the forward electron transfer lead to charge separation, electron back-transfer will result in charge recombination."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:B00575)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:C00998)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:C00999)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:E02011)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:ET07026)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:E02257)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:I03146)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07367 goldbook:O04365)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07367 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07367 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07368))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07368 "electrostriction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07368 "Contraction of a dielectric in an electric-field gradient.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07368 "For example, the contraction taking place around the charged centres created as a consequence of the sudden formation of separated positive and negative charges in the process of electron transfer."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07368 "The thermodynamic description of electrostriction was given by Drude and Nernst in 1894 to explain the phenomenon of contraction of a continuum medium, such as an alkane, around charges suddenly produced in that medium."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07368 goldbook:E02011)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07368 goldbook:G02669)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07368 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07368 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07369))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07369 "El-Sayed rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07369 "The rate of intersystem crossing is relatively large if the radiationless transition involves a change of orbital type.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07369 "A typical case is the transition from the lowest singlet state to the triplet manifold, e.g., 1π,π* → 3n, π* is faster than 1π, π* → 3π, π* and 1n, π* → 3π, π* is faster than 1n, π* → 3n, π*."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07369 goldbook:I03123)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07369 goldbook:R05057)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07369 goldbook:S05699)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07369 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07369 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07370))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07370 "emission anisotropy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07370 "Used to characterize luminescence (fluorescence, phosphorescence) polarization resulting from photoselection. Defined as: \\[r = \\frac{I_{\\parallel} - I_{\\perp }}{I_{\\parallel} + 2I_{\\perp }}\\] where \\(I_{\\parallel}\\) and \\(I_{\\perp }\\) are the intensities measured with the linear polarizer for emission parallel and perpendicular, respectively, to the electric vector of linearly polarized incident electromagnetic radiation (which is often vertical). The quantity \\(I_{\\parallel} + 2I_{\\perp }\\) is proportional to the total fluorescence intensity \\(I\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07370 "Fluorescence polarization may also be characterized by the polarization ratio, also called the degree of polarization \\(p\\), \\[p = \\frac{I_{\\parallel} - I_{\\perp }}{I_{\\parallel} + 2I_{\\perp }}\\] For parallel absorbing and emitting transition moments the (theoretical) values are \\((r,p) = \\left (^2/_5,^1/_2 \\right)\\); when the transition moments are perpendicular, the values are \\((r,p) = \\left ( -^1/_5,-^1/_3 \\right )\\). In many cases, it is preferable to use emission anisotropy because it is additive; the overall contribution of \\(n\\) components \\(r_{i}\\), each contributing to the total @F02453-2@ intensity with a fraction \\(f_{i} = I_{i}/I\\), is given by:\\(r = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f_{i}\\, r_{i}\\) with \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f_{i} = 1\\)"^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07370 "On continuous illumination, the measured emission anisotropy is called steady-state emission anisotropy (\\(\\bar{r}\\)) and is related to the time-resolved anisotropy by: \\[\\bar{r} = \\frac{\\int_{0}^{\\infty} r(t)\\, I(t)\\, \\rm{d}t}{\\int_{0}^{\\infty} I(t)\\, \\rm{d}t}\\] where \\(r(t)\\) is the anisotropy and \\(I(t)\\) is the @R05045@ of the emission, both at time \\(t\\) following a δ-pulse excitation."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07370 "Luminescence polarization spectroscopy, with linear polarizers placed in both beams, is usually performed on isotropic samples, but it may also be performed on oriented anisotropic samples. In the case of an anisotropic, uniaxial sample, five linearly independent luminescence spectra, instead of the two available for an isotropic sample, may be recorded by varying the two polarizer settings relative to each other and to the sample axis."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07370 "The term fundamental emission anisotropy describes a situation in which no depolarizing events occur subsequent to the initial formation of the emitting state, such as those caused by rotational diffusion or energy transfer. It also assumes that there is no overlap between differently polarized transitions. The (theoretical) value of the fundamental emission anisotropy, \\(r_{0}\\), depends on the angle \\(α\\) between the absorption and emission transition moments in the following way: \\[r_{0} =\\, \\! /5\\] where \\(\\) denotes an average over the orientations of the photoselected molecules. \\(r_{0}\\) can take on values ranging from \\(-1/5\\) for \\(\\alpha = \\pu{90\\!^{\\circ}}\\) (perpendicular transition moments) to \\(2/5\\) for \\(\\alpha = 0\\, °\\) (parallel transition moments). In spite of the severe assumptions, the expression is frequently used to determine relative transition-moment angles."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07370 "In time-resolved fluorescence with δ-pulse excitation, the theoretical value at time zero is identified with the fundamental emission anisotropy."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:A00134)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:A00346)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:AT06776)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:E02056)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:E02116)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:F02453)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:L03555)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:L03641)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:P04569)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:PT07461)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:P04712)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:R05045)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:R05410)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07370 goldbook:S05848)
