AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07370 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07371))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07371 "entrainment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07371 "Term used in photostimulated radical nucleophilic substitution to designate the induction of the reaction of a less reactive (or non-reactive) nucleophile with a substrate by the addition of catalytic amounts of another nucleophile, more reactive at initiation. Often, the non-reactive nucleophile at initiation is, however, quite reactive at propagation.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07371 "Term also used in photobiology in connection with the setting of the circadian clock by biological photosensors such as phytochromes and cryptochromes in plants and cryptochromes in mammals and insects."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07371 goldbook:I03042)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07371 goldbook:PT07445)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07371 goldbook:P04881)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07371 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07371 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:ET07372))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:ET07372 "excimer lamp")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:ET07372 "Non-coherent source of ultraviolet radiation capable of producing quasi-monochromatic radiation from the near UV (\\(\\lambda = 354\\ \\rm{nm}\\)) to the vacuum UV (\\(\\lambda = 126\\ \\rm{nm}\\)). The operation of the excimer lamps relies on the radiative decomposition of excimers or exciplexes created by various types of discharges.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07372 "Using noble gas, halogen, or noble gas/halogen mixtures with fill pressure \\(\\sim 30\\ \\rm{kPa}\\), the radiative decomposition of the excimer or the exciplex produces nearly monochromatic radiation. Some of the commercially available wavelengths for the particular excimers or exciplexes are \\(126\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with $\\ce{Ar2}$, \\(146\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with $\\ce{Kr2}$, \\(172\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with $\\ce{Xe2}$, \\(222\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with $\\ce{KrCl}$, and \\(308\\ \\rm{nm}\\) with $\\ce{XeCl}$, obtained with efficiencies of \\(5{-}15\\,\\%\\). Pulsed Xe-excimer ($\\ce{Xe2}$) lamps may have up to 40 % efficiency. Good efficiencies are also obtained with XeBr at \\(291\\ \\rm{nm}\\) and with XeI at \\(253\\ \\rm{nm}\\). Other wavelengths produced with much less efficiency are \\(207\\ \\rm{nm}\\) ($\\ce{KrBr}$), \\(253\\ \\rm{nm}\\) ($\\ce{XeI}$), \\(259\\ \\rm{nm}\\) ($\\ce{Cl2}$), and \\(341\\ \\rm{nm}\\) ($\\ce{I2}$) (see Table 1).Table 1: Peak wavelengths (\\(\\rm{nm}\\)) obtained in dielectric-barrier discharges with mixtures of noble gas ($\\ce{Ng}$) and halogen ($\\ce{X2}$). Wavelengths of commercially available lamps are shown in boldface type. The molecular species indicated are excimers or exciplexes.$\\ce{X2}$$\\ce{Ne}$$\\ce{Ar}$$\\ce{Kr}$$\\ce{Xe}$$\\ce{Ng2}$126146172$\\ce{F}$157108193249354$\\ce{Cl}$259175222308$\\ce{Br}$291165207283$\\ce{I}$341190253"^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:ET07372 "Phosphors are used to transform the UV radiation into visible radiation. This is the basis of mercury-free fluorescent lamps and of flat plasma-display panels with a large screen."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07372 goldbook:D01547)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07372 goldbook:E02242)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07372 goldbook:E02246)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07372 goldbook:UT07492)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:ET07372 goldbook:VT07496)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:ET07372 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:ET07372 "current"^^xsd:string)
