Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02580))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02580 "gaseous diffusion separator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02580 "Instrumentation to separate a gas mixture into its components by means of differences in the diffusion rates of the component molecules. This method has been used in separation of various isotopes of uranium (by means of $\\ce{UF6}$) and hydrogen ($\\ce{H2O}$, $\\ce{HDO}$, $\\ce{D2O}$, $\\ce{HTO}$, etc.)")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02580 goldbook:D01716)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02580 goldbook:I03331)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:G02580 doi:10.1351/pac199062112167)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:G02580 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02581))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02581 "gas-filled phototube")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02581 "Similar in construction to a vacuum phototube except that it is filled with a noble gas (usually $\\ce{Ar}$) at a pressure of about \\(10\\ \\rm{Pa}\\). Photoelectrons accelerated by the anode electric potential ionize gas atoms. The additional electrons provide a substantial intrinsic gain.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02581 goldbook:A00370)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02581 goldbook:E01935)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02581 goldbook:V06586)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:G02581 doi:10.1351/pac199567101745)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:G02581 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:G02582))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:G02582 "gas-filled X-ray detector")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:G02582 "A cylindrical cathode with a window, an axial wire anode and an ionizable gas. The gas may be continuously replenished giving a flow-through detector or the detector may be sealed. Following an original ionizing event, electron multiplication occurs through a process of gas amplification in the high electric field surrounding the anode wire. The gain of this process is defined as the number of electrons collected on the anode wire for each primary electron produced. For X-rays having energies higher than the excitation potential of the detector gas, the spectral responsivity function has a second peak in addition to the main peak that is called the escape peak. The escape peak has a mean pulse height proportional to the difference between the photon energy of the incident X-rays and of the spectral characteristic line of the detector gas. A quenching gas, a molecular gas, is added to the detector gas in order to neutralize the detector gas ions and to absorb secondary electrons as well as UV radiation resulting from neutralization of detector gas ions. According to the potential applied to the anode, the detector can work as an ionization chamber, proportional counter, or Geiger counter.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:G02598)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:A00370)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:C00905)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:I03190)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:P04885)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:Q05007)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:G02582 goldbook:R05347)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:G02582 doi:10.1351/pac199567101745)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:G02582 "current"^^xsd:string)
