AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:K03401 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03402))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03402 "kinetic energy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03402 "Energy of motion: for a body of mass \\(m\\), \\[E_{\\rm{k}}=\\frac{m\\ v^{2}}{2}\\] where \\(v\\) is the speed.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:K03402 doi:10.1351/pac199668040957)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:K03402 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03403))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03403 "kinetic equivalence")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03403 "Two reaction schemes are kinetically equivalent if they imply the same rate law. For example, consider the two schemes (i) and (ii) for the formation of $\\ce{C}$ from $\\ce{A}$: \\[(\\rm{i}) \\qquad \\ce{A [k_{1},\\ce{OH^{-}}][k_{-1},\\ce{OH^{-}}] B ->[k_{2}] C}\\] Providing that $\\ce{B}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate. \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{k_{1} k_{2}[\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{k_{2} + k_{-1} [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] \\[(\\rm{ii}) \\qquad \\ce{A [k_{1}][k_{-1}] B ->[k_{2}][\\ce{OH^{-}}] C}\\] Providing that $\\ce{B}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{k_{1} k_{2}[\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{k_{-1} + k_{2} [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] Both equations for \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t}\\) are of the form: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}[\\ce{C}]}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{r [\\ce{A}][\\ce{OH^{-}}]}{1 + s [\\ce{OH^{-}}]}\\] where \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are constants (sometimes called 'coefficients in the rate equation'). The equations are identical in their dependence on concentrations and do not distinguish whether $\\ce{OH^{−}}$ catalyses the formation of $\\ce{B}$, and necessarily also its reversion to $\\ce{A}$, or is involved in its further transformation to $\\ce{C}$. The two schemes are therefore kinetically equivalent under conditions to which the stated provisos apply.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:K03403 "kinetic ambiguity")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03403 goldbook:R05141)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03403 goldbook:R05171)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03403 goldbook:T06446)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:K03403 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:K03403 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:K03405))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:K03405 "kinetic isotope effect")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:K03405 "The effect of isotopic substitution on a rate constant is referred to as a kinetic isotope effect. For example, in the reaction:  the effect of isotopic substitution in reactant $\\ce{A}$ is expressed as the ratio of rate constants \\(\\frac{k^{\\rm{l}}}{k^{\\rm{h}}}\\), where the superscripts \\(\\rm{l}\\) and \\(\\rm{h}\\) represent reactions in which the molecules $\\ce{A}$ contain the light and heavy isotopes, respectively. Within the framework of transition state theory in which the reaction is rewritten as:  and with neglect of isotopic mass on tunnelling and the transmission coefficient, \\(\\frac{k^{\\rm{l}}}{k^{\\rm{h}}}\\) can be regarded as if it were the equilibrium constant for an isotope exchange reaction between the transition state $\\ce{[TS]^{\\ddagger}}$ and the isotopically substituted reactant $\\ce{A}$, and calculated from their vibrational frequencies as in the case of a thermodynamic isotope effect. Isotope effects like the above, involving a direct or indirect comparison of the rates of reaction of isotopologues, are called 'intermolecular', in contrast to intramolecular isotope effects, in which a single substrate reacts to produce a non-statistical distribution of isotopomeric product molecules.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:E02177)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03098)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03132)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03327)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03328)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03331)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03338)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03351)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:I03352)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:O04322)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:T06319)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:T06470)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:T06479)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:K03405 goldbook:T06531)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:K03405 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:K03405 "current"^^xsd:string)
