AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03491 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03492))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03492 "least-squares technique")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03492 "A procedure for replacing the discrete set of results obtained from an experiment by a continuous function. It is defined by the following. For the set of variables \\(y,x_{0},x_{1},\\,...\\) there are \\(n\\) measured values such as \\(y_{i},x_{0i},x_{1i},\\,...\\) and it is decided to write a relation: \\[y = f\\left(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K};x_{0},x_{1},\\,...\\right)\\] where \\(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K}\\) are undetermined constants. If it is assumed that each measurement \\(y_{i}\\) of \\(y\\) has associated with it a number \\(w_{i}^{-1}\\) characteristic of the uncertainty, then numerical estimates of the \\(a_{0},a_{1},\\,...,a_{K}\\) are found by constructing a variable \\(S\\), defined by \\[S = \\sum_{i}(w_{i}\\ (y_{i}- f_{i}))^{2}\\] and solving the equations obtained by writing \\[\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial a_{j}}\\ \\overset{˜}{a}_{j}=0\\] \\(\\overset{˜}{a}_{j}=\\rm{all}\\:\\, a\\) except \\(a_{j}\\). If the relations between the \\(a\\) and \\(y\\) are linear, this is the familiar least-squares technique of fitting an equation to a number of experimental points. If the relations between the \\(a\\) and \\(y\\) are non-linear, there is an increase in the difficulty of finding a solution, but the problem is essentially unchanged.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03492 goldbook:V06600)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03492 doi:10.1351/pac198153091805)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03492 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03493))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03493 "leaving group")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03493 "An atom or group (charged or uncharged) that becomes detached from an atom in what is considered to be the residual or main part of the substrate in a specified reaction. For example, in the heterolytic solvolysis of benzyl bromide in acetic acid:  the leaving group is $\\ce{Br^{−}}$; in the reaction:  the leaving group is $\\ce{NMe3}$ in the electrophilic nitration of benzene, it is $\\ce{H^{+}}$. The term has meaning only in relation to a specified reaction. The leaving group is not, in general, the same as the substituent group present in the substrate (e.g. bromo and trimethylammonio in the substrates of the first two examples above). A slightly different usage of the term prevails in the (non-mechanistic) naming of transformations, where the actual substituent group present in the substrate (and also in the product) is referred to as the leaving group.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:E01965)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:E02020)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:E02130)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:N04246)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:S05762)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:S06076)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03493 goldbook:S06082)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03493 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03493 "current"^^xsd:string)
