AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03565 "Determination of rate constants for a chemical exchange from the shapes of spectroscopic lines of dynamic processes. The method is most often used in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03565 goldbook:R05326)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03565 goldbook:S05848)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03565 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03565 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03566))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03566 "Lineweaver–Burk plot")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03566 "A plot of the reciprocal of velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction (ordinate) versus the reciprocal of substrate concentration (abscissa). The plot is used to graphically define the maximum velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction and the Michaelis constant for the enzyme.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03566 goldbook:M03891)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03566 goldbook:M03892)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03566 doi:10.1351/pac199466122587)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03566 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03568))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03568 "line width")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03568 "The full width at half maximum peak-height. The natural line width is the theoretical value of the full width at half maximum of the nuclear transition, usually calculated from lifetime data.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03568 goldbook:L03515)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03568 goldbook:N04241)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03568 doi:10.1351/pac197645030211)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03568 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03569))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03569 "linked scan")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03569 "A scan, in an instrument comprising two or more analysers, in which two or more of the analyser fields are scanned simultaneously so as to preserve a pre-determined relationship between parameters characterizing these fields. Often, these parameters are the field strengths, but they may also be the frequencies in the case of analysers in which alternating fields are employed.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03569 doi:10.1351/pac199163101541)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03569 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03570))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03570 "lipid film")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03570 "Established term for W/O/W films (i.e. oil film in water).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03570 goldbook:F02366)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03570 doi:10.1351/pac197231040577)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03570 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03571))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03571 "lipids")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03571 "A loosely defined term for substances of biological origin that are soluble in nonpolar solvents. They consist of saponifiable lipids, such as glycerides (fats and oils) and phospholipids, as well as nonsaponifiable lipids, principally steroids.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03571 goldbook:G02647)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03571 goldbook:P04559)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03571 goldbook:S06005)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03571 doi:10.1351/pac199567081307)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03571 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03572))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03572 "lipophilic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03572 "Literally 'fat-loving'. Applied to molecular entities (or parts of molecular entities) having a tendency to dissolve in fat-like (e.g. hydrocarbon) solvents.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03572 goldbook:H02906)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03572 goldbook:H02907)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03572 goldbook:L03655)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03572 doi:10.1351/pac197231040577)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03572 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03572 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03573))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03573 "lipophobic")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03573 "Literally, 'fat-rejecting'; the converse of lipophilic.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03573 goldbook:L03572)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03573 doi:10.1351/pac197231040577)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03573 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03574))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03574 "lipopolysaccharides")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03574 "Natural compounds consisting of a trisaccharide repeating unit (two heptose units and octulosonic acid) with oligosaccharide side chains and 3-hydroxytetradecanoic acid units (they are a major constituent of the cell walls of gram-negative bacteria).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03574 goldbook:C01281)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03574 goldbook:G02680)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03574 doi:10.1351/pac199567081307)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03574 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03575))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03575 "lipoproteins")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03575 "Clathrate complexes consisting of a lipid enwrapped in a protein host without covalent binding in such a way that the complex has a hydrophilic outer surface consisting of all the protein and the polar ends of any phospholipids.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03575 goldbook:H02906)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03575 goldbook:L03571)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03575 goldbook:P04559)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03575 goldbook:P04898)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03575 doi:10.1351/pac199567081307)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03575 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03576))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03576 "liposome")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03576 "Originally a lipid droplet in the endoplasmic reticulum of a fatty liver. Now applied to an artificially formed lipid droplet, small enough to form a relatively stable suspension in aqueous media and with potential use in drug delivery.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03576 goldbook:D01863)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03576 goldbook:S05900)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03576 goldbook:S06198)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03576 doi:10.1351/pac199365092003)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03576 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03577))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03577 "Lippman's equation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03577 "An equation which gives the electric charge per unit area of an interface (electrode): \\[(\\frac{\\partial \\gamma }{\\partial E_{\\rm{A}}})_{T,p,\\mu _{i} eq \\mu }=- Q_{\\rm{A}}\\] where \\(\\gamma \\) is the interfacial tension, \\(E_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the potential of a cell in which the reference electrode has an interfacial equilibrium with one of the ionic components of A, \\(Q_{\\rm{A}}\\) is the charge on unit area of the interface, \\(\\mu _{i}\\) is the chemical potential of the combination of species \\(i\\) whose net charge is zero, \\(T\\) is the thermodynamic temperature and \\(p\\) is the external pressure. Since more than one type of reference electrode may be chosen, more than one quantity \\(Q\\) may be obtained. Consequently \\(Q\\) cannot be considered as equivalent to the physical charge on a particular region of the interphase. It is in fact an alternative way of expressing a surface excess or combination of surface excess of charged species.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:C01032)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:E01923)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:I03082)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:I03088)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:I03118)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:R05229)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:S06171)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03577 goldbook:T06321)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03577 doi:10.1351/pac197437040499)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03577 doi:10.1351/pac198658030437)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03577 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03578))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03578 "liquid chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03578 "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a liquid. Liquid chromatography can be carried out either in a column or on a plane. Present-day liquid chromatography generally utilizing very small particles and a relatively high inlet pressure is often characterized by the term high-performance (or high-pressure) liquid chromatography, and the acronym HPLC.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:L03578 "LC")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03578 goldbook:C01075)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03578 goldbook:D01527)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03578 doi:10.1351/pac199365040819)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03578 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:L03579))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:L03579 "liquid-crystal transitions")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:L03579 "A liquid crystal is a molecular crystal with properties that are both solid- and liquid-like. Liquid crystals are composed predominantly of rod-like or disc-like molecules, that can exhibit one or more different, ordered fluid phases as well as the isotropic fluid; the translational order is wholly or partially destroyed but a considerable degree of orientational order is retained on passing from the crystalline to the liquid phase in a mesomorphic transition.Transition to a nematic phase.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a nematic phase in which the mean direction of the molecules is parallel or antiparallel to an axis known as the director.Transition to a cholesteric phase. A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a cholesteric phase in which there is simply a spiralling of the local orientational order perpendicular to the long axes of the molecules. Transition to a smectic state.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to yield a  smectic state in which there is a one-dimensional density wave which produces very soft/disordered layers.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03579 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03579 goldbook:LT06856)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:L03579 goldbook:M03847)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:L03579 doi:10.1351/pac199466030577)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:L03579 "current"^^xsd:string)
