Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07076))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07076 "natural atomic orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07076 "A valence-shell atomic orbital whose derivation involves diagonalising the localized block of the full density matrix of a given molecule associated with basis functions \\(\\chi _{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) on that atom. A distinguishing feature of NAOs is that they meet the simultaneous requirement of orthonormality and maximum occupancy. For isolated atoms, NAOs coincide with natural orbitals. In a polyatomic molecule the NAOs (in contrast to natural orbitals that become delocalised over all nuclear centres) mostly retain one-centre character, and thus are optimal for describing the molecular electron density around each atomic centre.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07076 "NAO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07076 goldbook:B00682)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07076 goldbook:E01986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07076 goldbook:V06588)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07076 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07076 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07077))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07077 "natural bond orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07077 "The orbital which is formed from natural hybrid orbitals. For a localized $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ between atoms $\\ce{A}$ and $\\ce{B}$, the NBO is: \\[\\sigma _{\\rm{AB}}=c_{\\rm{A}}\\ \\rm{h}_{\\rm{A}}+c_{\\rm{B}}\\ \\mathrm{h}_{\\rm{B}}\\] where \\(\\rm{h}_{\\rm{A}}\\) and \\(\\rm{h}_{\\rm{B}}\\) are the natural hybrids centered on atoms $\\ce{A}$ and $\\ce{B}$. NBOs closely correspond to the picture of localized bonds and lone pairs as basic units of molecular structure, so that is possible to conveniently interpret ab initio wave-functions in terms of the classical Lewis structure concepts by transforming these functions to NBO form.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07077 "NBO")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07077 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07077 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07078))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07078 "natural hybrid orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07078 "Symmetrically orthogonalised directed hybrid orbital derived through unitary transformation of natural atomic orbitals centred on a particular atom.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07078 "NHO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07078 goldbook:HT07049)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07078 goldbook:T06446)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07078 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07078 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07079))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07079 "natural orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07079 "The orbitals defined (P. Lowdin) as the eigenfunctions of the spinless one-particle electron density matrix. For a configuration interaction (CI) wave-function constructed from orbitals \\(\\varPhi \\), the electron density function, \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3C1}\\), is of the form: \\[\\unicode[Times]{x3C1} = \\sum_{i}\\sum _{j}a_{ij}\\,\\varPhi_{i}^{*}\\,\\varPhi_{j}\\] where the coefficients \\(a_{ij}\\) are a set of numbers which form the density matrix. The NOs reduce the density matrix \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3C1}\\) to a diagonal form: \\[\\unicode[Times]{x3C1} = \\sum _{k}b_{k}\\mathit{\\Phi}_{k}^{*}\\mathit{\\Phi}_{k}\\] where the coefficients \\(b_{k}\\) are occupation numbers of each orbital. The importance of natural orbitals is in the fact that CI expansions based on these orbitals have generally the fastest convergence. If a CI calculation was carried out in terms of an arbitrary basis set and the subsequent diagonalisation of the density matrix \\(\\rm{a}_{ij}\\) gave the natural orbitals, the same calculation repeated in terms of the natural orbitals thus obtained would lead to the wave-function for which only those configurations built up from natural orbitals with large occupation numbers were important.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003012) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07079 "NO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07079 goldbook:BT06999)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07079 goldbook:E01986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07079 goldbook:ET07024)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07079 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07079 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07080))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07080 "natural population analysis")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07080 "The analysis of the electron density distribution in a molecular system based on the orthonormal natural atomic orbitals. Natural populations, \\(n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) are the occupancies of the natural atomic orbitals. These rigorously satisfy the Pauli exclusion principle: \\(0 < n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right) < 2\\). The population of an atom \\(n\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\) is the sum of natural populations \\(n\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)=\\sum _{\\begin{array}{c} \\rm{A} \\end{array}}n_{i}\\left(\\rm{A}\\right)\\). A distinguished feature of the NPA method is that it largely resolves the basis set dependence problem encountered in the Mulliken population analysis method.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07080 "NPA")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07080 goldbook:PT07089)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07080 goldbook:BT06999)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07080 goldbook:E01986)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07080 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07080 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07081))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07081 "negative hyper-conjugation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07081 "The effect of donation of electron density from filled $\\ce{\\uppi\\!}$ or $\\ce{n_{\\uppi\\!}\\mbox{-}orbitals}$ to the symmetry adapted $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}orbital(s)}$ of $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!^{\\ast}\\mbox{-}bond(s)}$ of a molecular entity resulting in building $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}character}$ into bonds that nominally possess only $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}character}$. The consequences of the effect are, for example, in elongation of the $\\ce{C–F}$ bond in the $\\ce{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox{-}fluoroethyl}$ anion and stabilization of the anti-periplanar conformation of fluoromethylamine.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07081 goldbook:A00358)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07081 goldbook:A00381)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07081 goldbook:C01258)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07081 goldbook:E01986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07081 goldbook:T06406)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07081 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07081 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07082))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07082 "nodal plane")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07082 "A plane of a system in which the value of the orbital wave-function equals zero. This plane defines a region of zero electron density for the orbital.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07082 goldbook:E01986)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07082 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07082 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07083))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07083 "nonbonding molecular orbital")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07083 "A molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons does not contribute (or contributes insignificantly) to the binding energy of the molecule. Generally, a nonbonding MO represents the highest occupied molecular orbital of a molecule.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07083 "MO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07083 goldbook:BT07001)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07083 goldbook:M03996)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07083 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07083 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07084))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07084 "nonclassical structure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07084 "The structure of molecules or molecular ions that escapes description in terms of conventional rules of valency and stereochemistry. Nonclassical structures are characteristic of carbonium ions with hypercoordinated (see hypercoordination) carbon atoms, e.g., methanium ion 1, pyramidal dication $\\ce{C6H6^{2+}}$ 2 (isomeric to benzene dication), and the molecular species whose structure cannot be adequately represented by the equilibrium (2-norbornyl cation, 3) or resonance of two or more classical structures. From the stereochemical point of view, those structures are assigned to the nonclassical type for which all tetracoordinate carbon bonds extend into a single hemisphere, i.e., the valence angle of a carbon atom is greater than $\\pu{180\\!^{\\circ}}$. A hypothetical example is pyramidane, 4, the structure of which corresponds to a local minimum on the $\\ce{C5H4}$ potential energy surface.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:A00346)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:C00907)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:HT07051)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:I03291)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:R05326)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07084 goldbook:V06588)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07084 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07084 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07085))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07085 "non-Kekulé molecules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07085 "Molecules that are fully conjugated, but each of whose Kekulé structures contains at least two atoms that are not $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonded}$. Examples of non-Kekule hydrocarbons are trimethylenemethane (2-methylidenepropane-1,3-diyl) 1, m-benzodiquinomethane (benzene-1,2-diyldimethyl) 2, and 1,2,4,5 - tetramethylenebenzene (benzene-1,2,4,5-tetrayltetramethyl) 3.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07085 goldbook:H02889)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07085 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07085 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07086))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07086 "normalization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07086 "A mathematical procedure which ensures that the integral of the square of modulus of a wave-function over all space equals 1. The constant required to ensure that a wave-function is normalized is termed the normalization constant.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07086 doi:10.1351/pac199971101919)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07086 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07178 "non-linear optical polymer")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07178 "Polymer that exhibits an optical effect brought about by electromagnetic radiation such that the magnitude of the effect is not proportional to the irradiance.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07178 "An example of non-linear optical effects is the generation of higher harmonics of the incident light wave."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07178 "A polymer that exhibits a non-linear optical effect due to anisotropic electric susceptibilities when subjected to electric field together with light irradiation is called an electro-optical polymer. A polymer that exhibits electro-optical behavior combined with photoconductivity is called a photo-refractive polymer."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07178 goldbook:I03255)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07178 goldbook:N04197)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07178 goldbook:P04591)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07178 doi:10.1351/pac200476040889)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07178 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07243))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07243 "nanocomposite")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07243 "Composite in which at least one of the phase domains has at least one dimension of the order of nanometres.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07243 goldbook:CT07545)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07243 doi:10.1351/pac200779101801)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07243 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07272))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07272 "nucleation of phase separation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07272 "Initiation of phase domain formation through the presence of heterogeneities.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07272 "In a metastable region of a phase diagram, phase separation is initiated only by nucleation."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07272 goldbook:I03042)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07272 goldbook:M03872)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07272 goldbook:PT07286)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07272 goldbook:P04534)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07272 doi:10.1351/pac200476111985)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07272 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07304))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07304 "nanodomain morphology")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07304 "Morphology consisting of phase nanodomains.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07304 goldbook:MT07285)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07304 doi:10.1351/pac200476111985)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07304 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07425))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07425 "non-crossing rule")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07425 "Electronic states of the same symmetry cannot cross along a reaction coordinate.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07425 "Only applies to diatomic molecules and not to polyatomic molecules."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07425 goldbook:R05168)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07425 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07425 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07426))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07426 "non-linear optical techniques")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07426 "Collective name applied to techniques related to non-linear optical effects.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07426 "CARS")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07426 "Some of these spectroscopies are four-wave mixing, hole burning, photon echoes, time-resolved coherent anti-stokes Raman (CARS), transient grating and stimulated pumping. These techniques differ in the nature of the pulse sequence, the geometry and the choice of a spatial direction (phase matching), as well as the mode of detection."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07426 "Low order non-linear techniques, such as three-wave mixing, are related to the second-order polarization. For a random isotropic medium with inversion symmetry, the second-order polarization vanishes and then the lowest order optical non-linear techniques, as well as the higher order non-linear techniques are related to the third-order polarization and the corresponding 'hyper-susceptibility'."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:A00381)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:H02834)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:I03146)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:M03958)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:P04712)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:ST06775)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07426 goldbook:S06027)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07426 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07426 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07427))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07427 "Norrish–Yang reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07427 "Photochemical process leading to the formation of cyclobutanols from excited carbonyl compounds by intramolecular $\\ce{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox{-}hydrogen}$ abstraction (Norrish Type II photoreaction) and subsequent 1,4-diradical combination.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07427 goldbook:N04218)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07427 goldbook:A00045)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07427 goldbook:C00844)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07427 goldbook:I03130)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07427 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07427 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07428))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07428 "nucleophilic aromatic photosubstitution")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07428 "Photosubstitution in aromatic compounds with the participation of nucleophiles.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:NT07428 "While electrophilic substitution is the most common reaction on aromatic compounds in the ground state, nucleophilic substitution is typical for the excited states of these compounds. Some electrophilic aromatic photosubstitutions (upon excitation of the aromatic substrate) have been reported."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07428 goldbook:G02704)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:NT07428 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:NT07428 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:NT07521))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:NT07521 "nanogel")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:NT07521 "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\(1\\) to \\(100\\ \\rm{nm}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:NT07521 "gel nanoparticle")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07521 goldbook:E02191)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:NT07521 goldbook:G02600)
