Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04340))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04340 "osmolality")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04340 "Quotient of the negative natural logarithm of the rational activity of water and the molar mass of water.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:O04340 doi:10.1351/pac199668040957)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:O04340 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04342))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04342 "osmotic coefficient")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04342 "Quantity characterizing the deviation of the solvent from ideal behaviour referenced to Raoult's law. The osmotic coefficient on a molality basis is defined by: \\[\\phi =\\frac{\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}- \\mu _{\\rm{A}}}{R\\ T\\ M_{\\rm{A}}\\ \\sum _{\\begin{array}{c} i \\end{array}}m_{i}}\\] and on an amount fraction basis by: \\[\\phi =\\frac{\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}- \\mu _{\\rm{A}}}{R\\ T\\ \\ln x_{\\rm{A}}}\\] where \\(\\mu _{\\rm{A}}^{*}\\) and \\(\\mu _{\\rm{A}}\\) are the chemical potentials of the solvent as a pure substance and in solution, respectively, \\(M_{\\rm{A}}\\) is its molar mass, \\(x_{\\rm{A}}\\) its amount fraction, \\(R\\) the gas constant and \\(T\\) the temperaure. The latter osmotic coefficient is sometimes called the rational osmotic coefficient.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04342 goldbook:H02783)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04342 goldbook:C01124)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04342 goldbook:D01650)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04342 goldbook:G02579)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04342 goldbook:S05746)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:O04342 doi:10.1351/pac199466030533)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:O04342 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04343))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04343 "osmotic concentration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04343 "Product of the osmolality and the mass density of water. Formerly called osmolarity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:O04343 "osmolarity")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04343 goldbook:M03714)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04343 goldbook:O04340)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:O04343 doi:10.1351/pac199668040957)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:O04343 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:O04344))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:O04344 "osmotic pressure")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:O04344 "Excess pressure required to maintain osmotic equilibrium between a solution and the pure solvent separated by a membrane permeable only to the solvent: \\[\\mathit{\\Pi} = -\\frac{R\\ T}{V_{\\rm{A}}}\\ \\ln a_{\\rm{A}}\\] where \\(V_{\\mathrm{A}}\\), \\(a_{\\mathrm{A}}\\) are the partial molar volume and activity of solvent \\(\\rm{A}\\) for an incompressible fluid. For ideal dilute solutions, \\(\\mathit{\\Pi} = c_{\\rm{B}}\\ R\\ T=\\rho _{\\rm{B}}\\ \\frac{R\\ T}{M_{\\rm{B}}}\\), where entities \\(\\rm{B}\\) are individually moving solute molecules, ions, etc., regardless of their nature, \\(c_{\\rm{B}}\\), \\(\\rho _{\\mathrm{B}}\\) are the amount and mass concentration of the solutes, and \\(M_{\\mathrm{B}}\\) is the mass average molar mass of the solutes. The amount is sometimes expressed in osmol (meaning a mole of osmotically active entities), but this usage and the corresponding term osmolarity are discouraged.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04344 goldbook:MT06878)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04344 goldbook:M03980)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:O04344 goldbook:O04343)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:O04344 doi:10.1039/9781847557889)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:O04344 "current"^^xsd:string)
