Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07437))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07437 "optical parametric amplification")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07437 "Process of signal amplification by a parametric process and a pump wave.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:OT07437 "OPA")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:OT07437 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:OT07437 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:OT07438))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:OT07438 "optical parametric oscillator")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:OT07438 "Light source similar to a laser, but based on 'non-linear optical gain' from parametric amplification rather than on stimulated emission.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:OT07438 "OPO")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:OT07438 "The device is a powerful solid-state source of broadly tunable coherent radiation. It consists of a crystal, usually BBO ($\\ce{BaB2O4}$), located inside an optical resonator and pumped by a very intense laser beam (typically provided by a pulsed neodymium laser or a diode laser). The pump beam (wavelength \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{p}}\\) frequency \\(\\nu_{\\rm{p}}\\)) is partially converted into two coherent beams, the signal and the idler, with wavelengths (\\(\\lambda_{\\rm{s}}\\), \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{l}}\\)) and frequencies (\\(\\nu_{\\rm{s}}\\), \\(\\nu_{\\rm{l}}\\)) such that \\(\\nu_{\\rm{s}} + \\nu_{\\rm{i}} = \\nu_{\\rm{p}}\\). By simultaneous rotation of the crystal and adjustment of the optical resonator, the wavelength of the signal beam is continuously tunable, theoretically from \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{p}}\\) to \\(2 \\times \\lambda_{\\rm{p}}\\) and practically over a slightly more reduced range."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:OT07438 "For example, for \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{p}} = 355\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (3rd harmonic of a Nd:YAG laser), \\(\\nu_{\\rm{s}}\\) can be tuned from \\(400\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (with \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{i}} \\approx  3.15\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{m}\\)) up to \\(600\\ \\rm{nm}\\) (with \\(\\lambda_{\\rm{i}} \\approx 870\\ \\rm{nm}\\))."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:OT07438 "This 'splitting of one photon into two photons' is the reverse of the 'sum frequency mixing' used, for instance, to generate the 3rd harmonic of a laser emission by mixing in a convenient crystal the fundamental and the frequency doubled beams (a way to get the 3rd harmonic much more efficiently than by pure frequency tripling as described under harmonic frequency generation)."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:L03526)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:C01130)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:D01747)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:H02744)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:L03459)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:N04103)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:PT07440)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:S06015)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:OT07438 goldbook:W06659)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:OT07438 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:OT07438 "current"^^xsd:string)
