Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05142))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05142 "ratemeter")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05142 "An electronic sub-assembly which gives a continuous indication proportional to the average counting rate over a predetermined time interval (time constant).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05142 goldbook:C01379)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05142 doi:10.1351/pac198254081533)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05142 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05144))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05144 "rate of change of a quantity")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05144 "The time derivative \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}Q}{\\rm{d}t}\\) of the value of the quantity. The differential quotient may also be called derivative (or instantaneous) rate of change. Examples are: rate of change of mass, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m}{\\rm{d}t}\\); rate of change of amount of substance, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n}{\\rm{d}t}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05144 goldbook:A00297)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05144 doi:10.1351/pac199264101569)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05144 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05145))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05145 "rate of change ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05145 "The quotient of two rates where the quantities are of the same kind in the same system for different components: \\[\\frac{\\rm{d}Q_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}Q_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\] For finite time intervals, mean rate of change ratio is: \\[\\frac{\\Delta Q_{1}/\\Delta t}{\\Delta Q_{2}/\\Delta t}=\\frac{\\Delta Q_{1}}{\\Delta Q_{2}}\\ \\Delta t\\] Rate of change ratio has the dimension one. The denominator is often called the reference quantity. Examples are: mass rate ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}m_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}m_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\); amount of substance rate ratio, \\(\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{1}/\\rm{d}t}{\\rm{d}n_{2}/\\rm{d}t}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05145 goldbook:A00297)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05145 doi:10.1351/pac199264101569)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05145 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05146))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05146 "rate of consumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05146 "The rate of consumption of a specified reactant may be defined in two ways:As the negative of the time derivative of the amount of reactant; thus for a reactant B, present at any time in amount \\(n_{\\rm{B}}\\), the rate of its consumption may be defined as: \\[ u (n_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems. Here and elsewhere, when a rate is defined in terms of a time derivative, it must be understood that the definition relates to the process occurring in isolation. In a flow system there may be no actual changes with time, and the time derivative must be inferred. Such an inference is also required for a reaction occurring by a composite mechanism.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of consumption per unit volume; thus for a reactant B the rate of consumption, \\( u (c_{\\rm{B}}) \\) is given by: \\[ u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume \\(V\\) is constant this reduces to: \\[ u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d[B]}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\(n_{\\rm{B}} = [\\rm{B}]V\\) may be differentiated to give: \\[{\\rm{d}}c_{\\rm{B}} = V\\rm{d[B]} + \\rm{[B]d}V\\] and therefore: \\[ u (c_{\\rm{B}}) = -\\frac{\\rm{d[B]}}{\\rm{d}t} - \\frac{\\rm{[B]}}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}V}{\\rm{d}t}\\] In contrast to the rate of conversion and the rate of reaction, the rate of consumption of a reactant may be specified even for a reaction of time-dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry. The rate of consumption of a reactant is often called its rate of disappearance. However, the former expression is to be preferred since the word disappearance is not appropriately translatable into certain languages. When English is used the word disappearance might be reserved for cases where the reactant is almost completely removed.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:C01210)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:R05147)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:R05148)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:R05156)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:R05163)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05146 goldbook:S06026)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05146 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05146 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05147))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05147 "rate of conversion")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05147 "The rate of conversion for a reaction occurring in a closed system is defined as the time derivative of the extent of reaction: \\[\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\xi }=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi }{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] In view of the definition of extent of reaction it follows that with reference to any species in a reaction showing time-independent stoichiometry \\[\\overset{\\rm{.}}{\\xi }=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\xi }{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{1}{ u _{i}}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}n_{i}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\] where \\(n_{i}\\) is the amount of the species at any time and \\( u _{i}\\) is its stoichiometric coefficient.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05147 goldbook:C01124)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05147 goldbook:E02283)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05147 goldbook:S06021)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05147 goldbook:S06026)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05147 doi:10.1351/pac199264101569)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05147 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05147 doi:10.1351/pac199668040957)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05147 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05151))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05151 "rate of fluid consumption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05151 "The volume of fluid consumed by the nebulizer divided by time (in \\(\\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{L}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\)). Volume rate has also been called volume flow, flux, aspiration rate, aspiration flow or aspiration flux. The fluid consumed is either aspirated or injected. The volume rate of fluid consumption by nebulizers is usually between \\(10\\) and \\(100\\ \\unicode[Times]{x3BC}\\rm{L}\\ \\rm{s}^{-1}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05151 goldbook:CT01101)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05151 doi:10.1351/pac198658121737)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05151 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05152))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05152 "rate of formation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05152 "Like the rate of consumption, the rate of formation of a specified product may be defined in two ways:As the time derivative of the amount of a product. Thus for a product $\\ce{Y}$, present at any time in amount \\(n_{\\rm{Y}}\\). the rate of its formation may be given by: \\[ u (n_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of formation per unit volume, denoted \\( u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) \\): \\[ u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is constant this reduces to: \\[ u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{1}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}n_{\\rm{Y}}}{\\rm{d}t} = \\frac{\\rm{d[Y]}}{\\rm{d}t}\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\(n_{\\rm{Y}} = [\\rm{Y}]V\\) may be differentiated to give: \\[{\\rm{d}}n_{\\rm{Y}} = V\\rm{d[Y]} + \\rm{[Y]d}V\\] and the rate of formation becomes: \\[ u (c_{\\rm{Y}}) = \\frac{\\rm{d[Y]}}{\\rm{d}t} + \\frac{\\rm{[Y]}}{V}\\frac{\\rm{d}V}{\\rm{d}t}\\] A rate of formation may be specified even for a reaction of time dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05152 goldbook:R05146)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05152 goldbook:S06026)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05152 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05152 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05154))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05154 "rate of migration")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05154 "The distance of migration divided by time, sometimes called velocity of migration. The symbol \\(v\\) is also used for velocity.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05154 goldbook:M03920)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05154 doi:10.1351/pac199466040891)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05154 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05156))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05156 "rate of reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05156 "For the general chemical reaction: \\[\\ce{aA + bB -> pP + qQ + \\dots}\\] occurring under constant-volume conditions, without an appreciable build-up of reaction intermediates, the rate of reaction \\( u\\) is defined as: \\[ u = -\\frac{1}{a}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t} = -\\frac{1}{b}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{B]}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{p}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{P]}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{q}\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{Q]}}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] where symbols placed inside square brackets denote amount (or amount of substance) concentrations (conventionally expressed in units of \\(\\pu{mol dm-3}\\)). The symbols \\(R\\) and \\(r\\) are also commonly used in place of \\( u\\). It is recommended that the unit of time should always be the second. In such a case the rate of reaction differs from the rate of increase of concentration of a product P by a constant factor (the reciprocal of its coefficient in the stoichiometric equation, \\(p\\)) and from the rate of decrease of concentration of the reactant A by \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\). The quantity: \\[\\dot{\\xi} = \\frac{{\\rm{d}}\\xi}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] defined by the equation: \\[\\dot{\\xi} = -\\frac{1}{a} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{A}}}{{\\rm{d}}t}  = -\\frac{1}{b} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{B}}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{p} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{P}}}{{\\rm{d}}t} = \\frac{1}{q} \\frac{{\\rm{d}}n_{\\ce{Q}}}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\] (where \\(n_{\\ce{A}}\\) designates the amount of substance A, conventionally expressed in units of mole) may be called the 'rate of conversion' and is appropriate when the use of concentrations is inconvenient, e.g. under conditions of varying volume. In a system of constant volume, the rate of reaction is equal to the rate of conversion per unit volume throughout the reaction. For a stepwise reaction this definition of 'rate of reaction' (and 'extent of reaction', \\(\\xi \\)) will apply only if there is no accumulation of intermediate or formation of side products. It is therefore recommended that the term 'rate of reaction' be used only in cases where it is experimentally established that these conditions apply. More generally, it is recommended that, instead, the terms 'rate of disappearance' or 'rate of consumption' of A (i.e. \\(-\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{A}]}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\), the rate of decrease of concentration of A) or 'rate of appearance' of P (i.e. \\(\\frac{{\\rm{d}}[\\ce{P}]}{{\\rm{d}}t}\\), the rate of increase of concentration of product P) be used, depending on the concentration change of the particular chemical species that is actually observed. In some cases reference to the chemical flux observed may be more appropriate. The symbol \\( u\\) (without lettered subscript) should be used only for rate of reaction; \\( u\\) with a lettered subscript (e.g. \\( u_{\\ce{A}}\\)) refers to a rate of appearance or rate of disappearance (e.g. of the chemical species A).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05156 goldbook:A00297)
