AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05157 "ratio")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05157 "Quotient of quantities of the same kind for different components within the same system.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05157 doi:10.1351/pac198557091299)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05157 doi:10.1351/pac199668040957)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05157 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05158))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05158 "raw coke")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05158 "The term raw coke is equivalent to green coke although it is now used less frequently.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05158 goldbook:C01142)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05158 goldbook:G02697)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05158 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05158 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05160))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05160 "Rayleigh scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05160 "The scattering of light by particles and molecules which are much smaller than the wavelength of the light. In the ideal case, the process is one of pure dipole and induced dipole interactions with the electric field of the light wave. The scattering cross section for light of wavelength \\(\\lambda \\) is proportional to \\(\\lambda ^{-4}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05160 goldbook:S05487)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05160 goldbook:W06659)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05160 doi:10.1351/pac199062112167)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05160 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05161))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05161 "rayon-based carbon fibres")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05161 "Carbon fibres made from rayon (cellulose) precursor fibres.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:R05161 "Rayon-based carbon fibres have a more isotropic structure than similarly heat-treated polyacrylonitrile (PAN)- or mesophase pitch (MPP)-based carbon fibres. Their Young's modulus values are therefore drastically lower (\\(100\\ \\rm{GPa}\\); tensile strength \\(100\\ \\rm{MPa}\\)). Rayon-based carbon fibres can be transformed into anisotropic carbon fibres with high strength and Young's modulus values by hot-stretching treatment at temperatures of approximately \\(2800\\ \\rm{K}\\)."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05161 goldbook:C00831)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05161 goldbook:M03966)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05161 goldbook:I03353)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05161 goldbook:M03851)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05161 doi:10.1351/pac199567030473)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05161 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05163))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05163 "reactant")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05163 "A substance that is consumed in the course of a chemical reaction. It is sometimes known, especially in the older literature, as a 'reagent', but this term is better used in a more specialized sense as a test substance that is added to a system in order to bring about a reaction or to see whether a reaction occurs (e.g. an analytical reagent).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05163 "reagent")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05163 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05163 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05164))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05164 "reacting bond rules")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05164 "For an internal motion of a molecular entity corresponding to progress over a transition state (energy maximum), any change that makes the motion more difficult will lead to a new molecular geometry at the energy maximum, in which the motion has proceeded further. Changes that make the motion less difficult will have the opposite effect. (This rule corresponds to the Hammond principle).For an internal motion of a molecular entity that corresponds to a vibration, any change that tends to modify the equilibrium point of the vibration in a particular direction will actually shift the equilibrium in that direction.Effects on reacting bonds (bonds made or broken in the reaction) are the most significant. The bonds nearest the site of structural change are those most strongly affected.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05164 goldbook:H02734)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05164 goldbook:M04030)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05164 goldbook:M03986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05164 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05164 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05164 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05166))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05166 "reaction barrier")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05166 "The energy barrier to chemical reaction. In vibrationally adiabatic transition-state theory its height is the zero-point energy of the activated complex minus that of the reactants.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05166 goldbook:A00092)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05166 goldbook:A00146)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05166 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05166 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05167))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05167 "reaction chromatography")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05167 "A technique in which the identities of the sample components are intentionally changed between sample introduction and detection. The reaction can take place upstream of the column when the chemical identity of the individual components passing through the column differs from that of the original sample, or between the column and the detector when the original sample components are separated in the column but their identity is changed prior to entering the detection device.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05167 doi:10.1351/pac199365040819)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05167 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05168))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05168 "reaction coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05168 "A geometric parameter that changes during the conversion of one (or more) reactant molecular entities into one (or more) product molecular entities and whose value can be taken for a measure of the progress of an elementary reaction (for example, a bond length or bond angle or a combination of bond lengths and/or bond angles; it is sometimes approximated by a non-geometric parameter, such as the bond order of some specified bond). In the formalism of 'transition-state theory', the reaction coordinate is that coordinate in a set of curvilinear coordinates obtained from the conventional ones for the reactants which, for each reaction step, leads smoothly from the configuration of the reactants through that of the transition state to the configuration of the products. The reaction coordinate is typically chosen to follow the path along the gradient (path of shallowest ascent/deepest descent) of potential energy from reactants to products. The term has also been used interchangeably with the term transition coordinate, applicable to the coordinate in the immediate vicinity of the potential energy maximum. Being more specific, the name transition coordinate is to be preferred in that context.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:A00346)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:BT07003)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:B00707)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:E02035)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:G02669)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:M03931)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:M03986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:P04779)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:P04780)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:R05178)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:T06455)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05168 goldbook:T06470)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05168 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05168 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05168 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05169))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05169 "reaction cross-section")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05169 "A quantity used in collision theories of reactions in order to interpret calculated or experimental rates. In some collision theories the reaction cross-section is considered to be related to \\(b_{\\max }\\), the maximum value of the impact parameter that allows reaction to occur, by: \\[\\sigma _{\\rm{r}}=P_{\\rm{r}}\\ \\pi \\ b_{\\max }^{2}\\] where \\(P_{\\rm{r}}\\) is the reaction probability.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05169 goldbook:C01161)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05169 goldbook:I02987)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05169 goldbook:R05176)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05169 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05169 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05170))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05170 "reaction dynamics")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05170 "A branch of chemical kinetics that is concerned with the intermolecular and intramolecular motions that occur in the elementary act of chemical change, and with the details of the relationships between the quantum states of the reactant molecules and those of the product molecules. It is also known as molecular dynamics.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05170 goldbook:I03098)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05170 goldbook:I03130)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05170 goldbook:M03985)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05170 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05170 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05174))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05174 "reaction path")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05174 "A synonym for mechanism.A trajectory on the potential-energy surface.A sequence of synthetic steps.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05174 goldbook:M03804)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05174 goldbook:M03931)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05174 goldbook:P04780)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05174 goldbook:ST06775)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05174 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05174 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05175))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05175 "reaction path degeneracy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05175 "A factor that is introduced into rate theory to take account of the fact that the process may be able to occur in different but equivalent ways. Thus the process:  has a reaction path degeneracy of 2 since the chlorine atom can abstract either of the two hydrogen atoms in the hydrogen molecule. The reaction path degeneracy has also been referred to as the statistical factor for the reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05175 "statistical factor")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05175 goldbook:D01556)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05175 goldbook:R05174)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05175 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05175 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05176))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05176 "reaction probability")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05176 "The probability that a reaction occurs when two particles undergo a collision. If \\(N_{\\rm{c}}\\) is the number of collisions occurring in unit time, and \\(N_{\\rm{r}}\\) the number of reactions occurring in unit time: \\(P_{\\rm{r}}=\\frac{N_{\\rm{r}}}{N_{\\rm{c}}}\\).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05176 goldbook:P04855)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05176 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05176 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05177))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05177 "reaction stage")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05177 "A set of one or more (possibly experimentally inseparable) reaction steps leading to and/or from a detectable or presumed reaction intermediate.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05177 goldbook:I03096)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05177 goldbook:R05178)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05177 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05177 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05178))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05178 "reaction step")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05178 "An elementary reaction, constituting one of the stages of a stepwise reaction in which a reaction intermediate (or, for the first step, the reactants) is converted into the next reaction intermediate (or, for the last step, the products) in the sequence of intermediates between reactants and products.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:E02035)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:R05140)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:R05171)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:R05177)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:ST06775)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05178 goldbook:S05970)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05178 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05178 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05179))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05179 "reaction time")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05179 "The period of time that elapses between the start of the reaction and the attainment of a given extent of a reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05179 doi:10.1351/pac199365102291)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05179 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05180))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05180 "reactive")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05180 "As applied to a chemical species, the term expresses a kinetic property. A species is said to be more reactive or to have a higher reactivity in some given context than some other (reference) species if it has a larger rate constant for a specified elementary reaction. The term has meaning only by reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed set of conditions. It is not to be used for reactions or reaction patterns of compounds in general. The term is also more loosely used as a phenomenological description not restricted to elementary reactions. When applied in this sense the property under consideration may reflect not only rate, but also equilibrium, constants.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:CT01038)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:E02035)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:O04322)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:S05900)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:U06567)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05180 goldbook:U06569)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05180 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05180 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05181))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05181 "reactive adsorption")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05181 "Reactive adsorption (and its reverse, reactive desorption) resembles dissociative adsorption (and its reverse, dissociative adsorption) but one fragment adds to an adsorbate rather than to a surface site.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05181 goldbook:A00152)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05181 goldbook:D01803)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05181 doi:10.1351/pac197646010071)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05181 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05182))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05182 "reactive complex")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05182 "A species of very short life that occurs as an intermediate in a chemical reaction. An activated complex (or transition state) is a special case.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05182 goldbook:A00092)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05182 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05182 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05182 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05184))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05184 "reactive scattering")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05184 "If in a molecular-beam experiment a chemical reaction occurs there is said to be reactive scattering.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05184 goldbook:M03982)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05184 goldbook:S05487)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05184 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05184 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05185))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05185 "reactivity index")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05185 "Any numerical index derived from quantum mechanical model calculations that permits the prediction of relative reactivities of different molecular sites. Many indices are in use, based on a variety of theories and relating to various types of reaction. The more successful applications have been to the substitution reactions of conjugated systems where relative reactivities are determined largely by changes of $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}electron}$ energy.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05185 goldbook:C01267)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05185 goldbook:S06078)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05185 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05185 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:R05186))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:R05186 "reactivity–selectivity principle")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:R05186 "This idea may be expressed loosely as: the more reactive a reagent is, the less selective it is. Consider two substrates \\(\\rm{S}^{1}\\) and \\(\\rm{S}^{2}\\) undergoing the same type of reaction with two reagents \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) and \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\), \\(\\rm{S}^{2}\\) being more reactive than \\(\\rm{S}^{1}\\), and \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) more reactive than \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) in the given type of reaction. The relative reactivities (in log units) for the four possible reactions may notionally be represented as shown in the diagram.  With the positions of (\\(\\rm{S}^{1}+\\rm{R}^{1}\\)), (\\(\\rm{S}^{2}+\\rm{R}^{1}\\)) and (\\(\\rm{S}^{1}+\\rm{R}^{2}\\)) fixed, there are three types of positions for (\\(\\rm{S}^{2}+\\rm{R}^{2}\\)). In position (i) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(a\\), is the same as the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, also \\(a\\). In position (ii) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(b\\), is less than the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, i.e. \\(b < a\\). It is this situation which is in accord with the RSP. In position (iii) the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{2}\\) for the two substrates, measured by \\(c\\), is greater than the selectivity of \\(\\rm{R}^{1}\\) for the two substrates, i.e. \\(c > a\\). This situation may be described as anti-RSP. There are many examples in which the RSP is followed, but there are also many examples corresponding to situations (i) and (iii). The RSP is in accord with intuitive feeling and certainly holds in the limiting case when reactivity is controlled by diffusion. However, the validity of the RSP is a matter of great controversy.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType OMO:0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:R05186 "RSP")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05186 goldbook:A00381)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05186 goldbook:D01716)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05186 goldbook:R05180)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05186 goldbook:R05190)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:R05186 goldbook:S05563)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:R05186 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:R05186 "current"^^xsd:string)
