Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06447))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06447 "transformation")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06447 "A process for genetic alteration of a cell following incorporation of foreign DNA.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06447 goldbook:D01597)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06447 doi:10.1351/pac199264010143)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06447 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06450))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06450 "transient phase")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06450 "This expression relates to the period of time that elapses prior to the establishment of a steady state. Initially the concentration of a reactive intermediate is zero, and it rises to the steady-state concentration during the transient phase.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06450 "induction period")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06450 goldbook:S05962)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06450 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06450 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06451))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06451 "transient species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06451 "Relating to a short-lived reaction intermediate. It can be defined only in relation to a time scale fixed by the experimental conditions and the limitations of the technique employed in the detection of the intermediate. The term is a relative one. Transient species are sometimes also said to be 'metastable'. However, this latter term should be avoided, because it relates a thermodynamic term to a kinetic property, although most transients are also thermodynamically unstable with respect to reactants and products.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06451 "transient chemical species")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06451 goldbook:I03096)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06451 goldbook:M03872)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06451 goldbook:P04516)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06451 goldbook:U06569)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06451 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06451 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06453))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06453 "transient spectroscopy")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06453 "A technique for the spectroscopic observation of transient species (excited-state molecular entities or reactive intermediates) generated by a pulse of short duration.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06453 goldbook:F02418)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06453 goldbook:T06385)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06453 doi:10.1351/pac199668122223)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06453 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06455))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06455 "transition coordinate")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06455 "The reaction coordinate at the transition state corresponding to a vibration with an imaginary frequency. Motion along it in the two opposite senses leads towards the reactants or towards the products.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06455 goldbook:R05168)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06455 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06455 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06455 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06456))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06456 "transition element")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06456 "An element whose atom has an incomplete d sub-shell, or which can give rise to cations with an incomplete d sub-shell.")
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06456 doi:10.1515/pac-2014-0718)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06456 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06460))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06460 "transition moment")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06460 "An electromagnetic wave may induce an oscillating electric moment in a molecule (possibly leading to absorption if the oscillation frequency is equal to the light frequency). The amplitude of this moment is the transition moment between the initial (i) and final (f) states (here assumed to be non-degenerate): \\[M_{\\rm{if}} = \\, < \\rm{f} \\mid M_{\\rm{op}}\\mid \\rm{i} >\\] where \\(M_{\\rm{op}}\\) is the electric dipole moment operator, a vector operator that is the sum of the position vectors of all charged particles weighted with their charge. The transition moment \\(M_{\\rm{if}} \\) is a vector in the molecular framework, characterized both by its direction and its probability.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06460 "transition dipole moment")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06460 "electronic transition moment")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:T06460 "The absorption probability for linearly polarized light is proportional to the cosine square of the angle between the electric vector of the electromagnetic wave and \\(M_{\\rm{if}} \\); light absorption will be maximized if they are parallel, and no absorption will occur if they are perpendicular."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:T06460 "It is frequently said that a transition is polarized along the direction of its transition moment and this direction is called the polarization direction of the transition."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:T06460 "In the case of a doubly degenerate final state f, each of the two components at the same energy has a transition moment and the two moments define a plane. The transition is then said to be polarized in that plane, which also defines its polarization direction(s). This is typically the case for some of the transitions in highly symmetrical molecules."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment goldbook:T06460 "In the case of a vibronic transition, where both the initial and the final states may be characterized by (different) electronic and vibrational states, the Franck–Condon principle is often applied. This approximation separates electronic and nuclear descriptions and allows the transition moment to be written as a product of a purely electronic transition moment and an overlap integral between the two vibrational wavefunctions involved."^^xsd:string)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:F02510)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:A00346)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:E01929)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:O04357)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:P04712)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:P04855)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06460 goldbook:V06618)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06460 doi:10.1351/pac200779030293)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06460 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06462))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06462 "transition polarization")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06462 "The direction of the transition moment in the molecular framework.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06462 goldbook:T06460)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06462 doi:10.1351/pac199668122223)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06462 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06467))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06467 "transition species")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06467 "Any intermediate species in an elementary reaction. An activated complex is an example of a transition species, but the latter term also includes other intermediate species. Transition species have also been called transition states, but there is then danger of confusion with a true activated complex, which is also commonly known as a transition state.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06467 goldbook:A00092)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06467 goldbook:E02035)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06467 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06467 doi:10.1351/pac199668010149)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06467 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06468))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06468 "transition state")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06468 "In theories describing elementary reaction it is usually assumed that there is a transition state of more positive molar Gibbs energy between the reactants and the products through which an assembly of atoms (initially composing the molecular entities of the reactants) must pass on going from reactants to products in either direction. In the formalism of 'transition state theory' the transition state of an elementary reaction is that set of states (each characterized by its own geometry and energy) in which an assembly of atoms, when randomly placed there, would have an equal probability of forming the reactants or of forming the products of that elementary reaction. The transition state is characterized by one and only one imaginary frequency. The assembly of atoms at the transition state has been called an activated complex. (It is not a complex according to the definition in this Compendium.) It may be noted that the calculations of reaction rates by the transition state method and based on calculated potential-energy surfaces refer to the potential energy maximum at the saddle point, as this is the only point for which the requisite separability of transition state coordinates may be assumed. The ratio of the number of assemblies of atoms that pass through to the products to the number of those that reach the saddle point from the reactants can be less than unity, and this fraction is the 'transmission coefficient' \\(\\kappa \\). (There are also reactions, such as the gas-phase colligation of simple radicals, that do not require 'activation' and which therefore do not involve a transition state.)")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:G02631)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:H02734)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:A00092)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:A00093)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:C01153)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:C01203)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:E02035)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:M03986)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:P04779)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:P04780)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:P04855)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:R05066)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:C01145)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:T06470)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06468 goldbook:T06471)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06468 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06468 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06469))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06469 "transition state analogue")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06469 "A substrate designed to mimic the properties or the geometry of the transition state of reaction.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06469 goldbook:T06468)
AnnotationAssertion(mito:isMentionedBy goldbook:T06469 doi:10.1351/pac199466051077)
AnnotationAssertion(obo:goldbook#hasStatus goldbook:T06469 "current"^^xsd:string)
Declaration(Class(goldbook:T06470))
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label goldbook:T06470 "transition state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:description goldbook:T06470 "A theory of the rates of elementary reactions which assumes a special type of equilibrium, having an equilibrium constant \\(K^{\\ddagger }\\), to exist between reactants and activated complexes. According to this theory the rate constant is given by: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ K^{\\ddagger }\\] where \\(k_{B}\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(h\\) is the Planck constant. The rate constant can also be expressed as: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ \\exp (\\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R})\\ \\exp (- \\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R\\ T})\\] where \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), the entropy of activation, is the standard molar change of entropy when the activated complex is formed from reactants and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), the enthalpy of activation, is the corresponding standard molar change of enthalpy. The quantities \\(E_{a}\\) (the energy of activation) and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) are not quite the same, the relationship between them depending on the type of reaction. Also: \\[k=\\frac{k_{\\rm{B}}\\ T}{h}\\ \\exp (- \\frac{\\Delta ^{\\ddagger }G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}}{R\\ T})\\] where \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), known as the Gibbs energy of activation, is the standard molar Gibbs energy change for the conversion of reactants into activated complex. A plot of standard molar Gibbs energy against a reaction coordinate is known as a Gibbs-energy profile; such plots, unlike potential-energy profiles, are temperature-dependent. In principle the equations above must be multiplied by a transmission coefficient, \\(\\kappa \\), which is the probability that an activated complex forms a particular set of products rather than reverting to reactants or forming alternative products. It is to be emphasized that \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}S^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\), \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}H^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) and \\(\\Delta ^{\\ddagger}G^{\\,\\unicode{x26ac}}\\) occurring in the former three equations are not ordinary thermodynamic quantities, since one degree of freedom in the activated complex is ignored. Transition-state theory has also been known as absolute rate theory, and as activated-complex theory, but these terms are no longer recommended.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym goldbook:T06470 "conventional transition-state theory")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06470 goldbook:B00695)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06470 goldbook:G02631)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06470 goldbook:P04685)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso goldbook:T06470 goldbook:A00092)
